sketchBlocks.bodyMarkdown:
JavaScript programs work with **values**: pieces of data the program can use. For now, focus on three kinds you will see constantly: strings for text, numbers for quantities, and booleans for `true` or `false`.

You can observe a value with `console.log(...)`:

```js
console.log("Campus Movie Night");
console.log(24);
console.log(true);
```

First, the quoted text is a **string**. Second, `24` is a **number**. Third, `true` is a **boolean**. `console.log` does not change those values; it simply shows them in the Console so you can see what the program is working with.

JavaScript also has values for “nothing here.” `null` is an intentional empty value, while `undefined` often means a value has not been provided yet. You only need to recognize those names for now; later lessons will give them more context.

An **expression** is code that JavaScript evaluates to produce a value. Arithmetic is a simple example:

```js
console.log(18 + 6);
console.log(30 - 12);
```

On the first line, JavaScript evaluates `18 + 6` and produces `24`. On the second line, it evaluates `30 - 12` and produces `18`. The Console shows the result of each expression.

You can also ask JavaScript what kind of value it sees:

```js
console.log(typeof "24");
console.log(typeof 24);
```

The first line prints `string` because the quotes make `"24"` text. The second prints `number` because `24` is numeric. The characters may look similar to us, but JavaScript treats their types differently. That distinction will matter when you start storing and combining values.

